      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1764505781509,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Key Concepts on the go \n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Key Concepts on the go \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Y3jegDnt_wNByJWdgpEyA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1678.8002341171255,
      "y": 98.87884180800614,
      "width": 936.6849612241604,
      "height": 775,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 526339248,
      "version": 6691,
      "versionNonce": 823055922,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1764648568067,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) So we are using cursor to page through the feed. Here we are required to give posts in\nchronological order hence we are using timestamp based cursor to represent the oldest post\nthey've seen so far, so each page will return N posts older than that timestamp.\n2) Database pick reasoning: Since we are looking for high scalability & availability we are going\nto use noSql database. \n3) For the case of following and social circle we could have used graphDb if the problem\nrequired more advance functionalities like finding second lvl connections, recommendations or\nmore. \n4) Key concept on NoSql data Access Patterns\n    - Partition Key (PK): This determines where the data lives. The database hashes this key\nto decide which physical server (node) stores the data.\n    - Sort Key (SK): This determines the order of data inside that specific partition.\n    - Global Secondary Index (GSI): A GSI effectively creates a copy of your data in a new\ntable structure, maintained automatically by the database, but with the keys \"flipped.\"\n    - Fan Out: the process of distributing a new post to all the followers' news feeds.\n        a) Fan-out on write (push model): Best for normal users; when Alice creates a post, the\nsystem finds all her followers (e.g., Bob, Charlie) and pushes the post ID into each follower’s\npre-computed news feed list so that when Bob opens the app his feed loads instantly, giving\nsuper fast reads but causing slow writes because a user with 1M followers triggers 1M\nimmediate writes.​\n\n        b) Fan-out on read (pull model): Best for celebrities or hot accounts; when Justin Bieber\ncreates a post the system just stores it once and does not push it to all followers, and when\na follower like Bob opens the app the system pulls recent posts from everyone he follows and\nbuilds the feed on the fly, giving fast writes but slower reads since the system must work\nharder on every feed request.\n\n\n\n\n    ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) So we are using cursor to page through the feed. Here we are required to give posts in chronological order hence we are using timestamp based cursor to represent the oldest post they've seen so far, so each page will return N posts older than that timestamp.\n2) Database pick reasoning: Since we are looking for high scalability & availability we are going to use noSql database. \n3) For the case of following and social circle we could have used graphDb if the problem required more advance functionalities like finding second lvl connections, recommendations or more. \n4) Key concept on NoSql data Access Patterns\n    - Partition Key (PK): This determines where the data lives. The database hashes this key to decide which physical server (node) stores the data.\n    - Sort Key (SK): This determines the order of data inside that specific partition.\n    - Global Secondary Index (GSI): A GSI effectively creates a copy of your data in a new table structure, maintained automatically by the database, but with the keys \"flipped.\"\n    - Fan Out: the process of distributing a new post to all the followers' news feeds.\n        a) Fan-out on write (push model): Best for normal users; when Alice creates a post, the system finds all her followers (e.g., Bob, Charlie) and pushes the post ID into each follower’s pre-computed news feed list so that when Bob opens the app his feed loads instantly, giving super fast reads but causing slow writes because a user with 1M followers triggers 1M immediate writes.​\n\n        b) Fan-out on read (pull model): Best for celebrities or hot accounts; when Justin Bieber creates a post the system just stores it once and does not push it to all followers, and when a follower like Bob opens the app the system pulls recent posts from everyone he follows and builds the feed on the fly, giving fast writes but slower reads since the system must work harder on every feed request.\n\n\n\n\n    ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
